WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:127826

1
00:00:00.867 --> 00:00:03.837
In this lesson
we get an overview of structured outputs.

2
00:00:03.837 --> 00:00:06.840
Why they are important and the different
approaches to generating them.

3
00:00:07.207 --> 00:00:10.844
You'll see how structured outputs
allow for scalable software development

4
00:00:10.844 --> 00:00:14.214
with LLMs. You'll learn
how the various approaches to structured

5
00:00:14.214 --> 00:00:17.717
outputs will lead you from prompt
hacking to true AI engineering.

6
00:00:18.251 --> 00:00:19.619
Let's dive in.

7
00:00:19.619 --> 00:00:22.889
Let's start by saying what we mean
when we say structured outputs.

8
00:00:23.223 --> 00:00:24.357
We work with an LLM,

9
00:00:24.357 --> 00:00:27.560
typically the output of the LLM is in
some sort of free-form text.

10
00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:29.429
There's no particular structure to that,

11
00:00:29.429 --> 00:00:30.864
it's just text.

12
00:00:30.864 --> 00:00:31.664
Structured outputs

13
00:00:31.664 --> 00:00:35.201
just mean we have some structure
that is adhered to by the model.

14
00:00:35.201 --> 00:00:36.269
In this case JSON.

15
00:00:36.269 --> 00:00:39.472
JSON is a very common form of structured
output, but there are many others.

16
00:00:40.006 --> 00:00:41.307
An obvious first question

17
00:00:41.307 --> 00:00:42.942
when you're working with structured
outputs is

18
00:00:42.942 --> 00:00:45.078
why do we need to use these
in the first place?

19
00:00:45.078 --> 00:00:48.248
If you've been using LLMs for a long time,
you're probably very familiar

20
00:00:48.248 --> 00:00:49.916
with the common chat interface.

21
00:00:49.916 --> 00:00:50.850
In this format,

22
00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:53.820
the model interacts with us as though
it's a human being talking to us.

23
00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:57.490
Typically, an assistant has a prompt,
we follow up with a question, and we have.

24
00:00:57.490 --> 00:01:01.361
In this case, I'm asking it to provide a
response to a social media message I got,

25
00:01:01.561 --> 00:01:03.496
and the model follows up with an answer.

26
00:01:03.496 --> 00:01:06.933
As human beings, it's very easy to parse
these responses, understand

27
00:01:06.933 --> 00:01:09.335
what the model is telling
us, and use that information.

28
00:01:09.335 --> 00:01:12.572
Let's take a look at this
in a programing environment.

29
00:01:12.705 --> 00:01:16.576
When working with LLMs in code
we use a very similar interface

30
00:01:16.576 --> 00:01:18.044
called the instruct interface.

31
00:01:18.044 --> 00:01:21.081
This mirrors the chat interface,
but it's slightly different.

32
00:01:21.081 --> 00:01:24.684
So we're actually sending in request
to the model and getting a response.

33
00:01:24.684 --> 00:01:27.120
This is the same content
we had in the last slide,

34
00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:28.588
but it's just in a different format.

35
00:01:28.588 --> 00:01:30.824
And once again
we get the response from the model.

36
00:01:30.824 --> 00:01:33.259
So this allows us to work
with the model in code.

37
00:01:33.259 --> 00:01:35.628
But we still need to work
with the response from the model.

38
00:01:35.628 --> 00:01:37.730
How are we going to parse out
this information

39
00:01:37.730 --> 00:01:39.866
that the model has sent back to us?
As human beings,

40
00:01:39.866 --> 00:01:42.001
it's very easy to do this,
but we need to be able to do this

41
00:01:42.001 --> 00:01:42.902
programmatically.

42
00:01:42.902 --> 00:01:45.472
Let's talk a little bit
about building systems with LLMs,

43
00:01:45.472 --> 00:01:48.174
so we can better understand
the need for structured outputs

44
00:01:48.174 --> 00:01:50.610
and how they help us build
scalable software.

45
00:01:50.610 --> 00:01:53.613
Let's build an LLM-empowered
social media agent.

46
00:01:53.613 --> 00:01:56.516
This agent is going to take responses

47
00:01:56.516 --> 00:01:57.984
we've gotten on social media

48
00:01:57.984 --> 00:01:59.252
and write its own response

49
00:01:59.252 --> 00:02:02.322
to them to automate this process
of handling our social media accounts.

50
00:02:02.489 --> 00:02:04.457
We have a very simple application here.

51
00:02:04.457 --> 00:02:05.959
We have an LLM with the prompt.

52
00:02:05.959 --> 00:02:08.661
We're going to append to it
the messages from our users.

53
00:02:08.661 --> 00:02:13.099
And we're going to get the response as the
LLM pass it into our social media API,

54
00:02:13.099 --> 00:02:15.301
and then it will post the response
on our social media.

55
00:02:15.301 --> 00:02:17.904
But the question is,
how are we going to handle this raw data?

56
00:02:17.904 --> 00:02:21.341
As we mentioned earlier, we need some way
to actually pull this data out.

57
00:02:21.808 --> 00:02:25.245
A naive solution to this problem
is to actually just add a layer

58
00:02:25.245 --> 00:02:26.045
that parses this.

59
00:02:26.045 --> 00:02:29.282
This is a very common
first pass at handling this problem

60
00:02:29.282 --> 00:02:32.051
when working with LLMs
is we'll just write some simple rules

61
00:02:32.051 --> 00:02:33.853
that will allow us
to parse out the answer.

62
00:02:33.853 --> 00:02:36.222
Unfortunately,
this is extremely time-consuming.

63
00:02:36.222 --> 00:02:38.591
if you've ever handwritten a parser
like this,

64
00:02:38.591 --> 00:02:40.460
you'll notice
that it takes a long time to write this,

65
00:02:40.460 --> 00:02:43.363
and that's partly
because the process is very error-prone.

66
00:02:43.363 --> 00:02:46.366
It's very easy to miss things,
or make mistakes, or find those outputs

67
00:02:46.366 --> 00:02:47.534
you weren't expecting.

68
00:02:47.534 --> 00:02:49.169
And this can be very tricky to get right.

69
00:02:49.169 --> 00:02:51.704
And because of this, it's
not easily extendable.

70
00:02:51.704 --> 00:02:54.707
To see this, let's
look at a different version of our agent.

71
00:02:54.741 --> 00:02:55.508
So suppose we have a

72
00:02:55.508 --> 00:02:59.245
PM that comes in and requests
that we modify our social media agent.

73
00:02:59.512 --> 00:03:02.515
They want it so that if the response from
an user is a complaint,

74
00:03:02.682 --> 00:03:05.084
we're going to send that complaint
to customer support.

75
00:03:05.084 --> 00:03:07.253
We don't want our model
automatically replying to people

76
00:03:07.253 --> 00:03:08.188
that are having a problem.

77
00:03:08.188 --> 00:03:11.224
We want to use a human being
to make sure we like the response.

78
00:03:11.257 --> 00:03:14.027
But for all other comments or messages
to us,

79
00:03:14.027 --> 00:03:16.563
we can go ahead
and have our agent respond to them.

80
00:03:16.563 --> 00:03:19.566
This requires
a simple forking of our process.

81
00:03:19.566 --> 00:03:20.900
This seems easy to implement,

82
00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:24.537
but using our technique from before,
we find it gets actually quite messy.

83
00:03:24.637 --> 00:03:29.309
Now we need to parse out both the is it
or is it not a complaint

84
00:03:29.309 --> 00:03:32.779
as well as our original parsing
out of the response itself if we need it.

85
00:03:32.879 --> 00:03:36.182
Clearly, this is not a scalable solution
to writing software with the LLMs.

86
00:03:36.749 --> 00:03:39.385
But what if we can have predictable JSON
in the output?

87
00:03:39.385 --> 00:03:40.787
In this case, it's very simple

88
00:03:40.787 --> 00:03:43.823
to implement this. We have a complaint
field and a response field.

89
00:03:44.090 --> 00:03:46.159
If the complaint field is true,
which we can check

90
00:03:46.159 --> 00:03:49.562
just like we check any other JSON,
we can send it to customer support.

91
00:03:49.762 --> 00:03:50.930
If it's not a complaint,

92
00:03:50.930 --> 00:03:54.367
we can go ahead and pass the response
to our social media API.

93
00:03:54.367 --> 00:03:57.370
And it's easy for the API
to extract this using JSON.

94
00:03:57.570 --> 00:04:00.573
The next question is,
how do we get structured outputs?

95
00:04:01.007 --> 00:04:03.543
The easiest way to get started with
structured outputs is working

96
00:04:03.543 --> 00:04:04.777
with the proprietary APIs

97
00:04:04.777 --> 00:04:06.646
you probably already use. Every inference

98
00:04:06.646 --> 00:04:10.583
provider offers a different solution
to how they provide structured outputs.

99
00:04:10.883 --> 00:04:12.885
We're going to talk about those
a little bit.

100
00:04:12.885 --> 00:04:16.489
One method of doing this is called logit
based methods or constrained decoding.

101
00:04:16.856 --> 00:04:21.094
This is when the model itself is modified
to only allow tokens to be generated

102
00:04:21.094 --> 00:04:22.895
that meet the requirements
of the structure.

103
00:04:22.895 --> 00:04:25.531
We're going to talk more about this
at the end of the lesson.

104
00:04:25.531 --> 00:04:28.534
Another common practice is called function-calling
or tool use.

105
00:04:28.801 --> 00:04:32.372
When doing function-calling, the models
provided with a list of functions

106
00:04:32.372 --> 00:04:36.276
that it can call or respond with that
are usually done in JSON format.

107
00:04:36.776 --> 00:04:39.445
There's also something called JSON mode,
where a model is simply

108
00:04:39.445 --> 00:04:42.415
fine tuned to return JSON,
and when prompted to do so.

109
00:04:42.715 --> 00:04:45.718
And of course, there always may be
some magic we don't understand.

110
00:04:45.952 --> 00:04:47.153
This is actually part of the problem

111
00:04:47.153 --> 00:04:49.322
with proprietary APIs,
because we don't have access

112
00:04:49.322 --> 00:04:51.491
to what they're doing behind the scenes
all the time.

113
00:04:51.491 --> 00:04:54.027
So let's talk about the pros
and cons of working with proprietary

114
00:04:54.027 --> 00:04:56.062
structured outputs from APIs.

115
00:04:56.062 --> 00:04:59.299
The pros are we are working with JSON,
which is great.

116
00:04:59.365 --> 00:05:02.368
We were working with unstructured text
before and we have JSON now.

117
00:05:02.368 --> 00:05:06.306
There's a huge leap forward as far
as creating reliable systems with the LLMs.

118
00:05:06.639 --> 00:05:08.474
These techniques are also easy to use

119
00:05:08.474 --> 00:05:10.810
if you're already
using one of these major providers.

120
00:05:10.810 --> 00:05:13.579
If you're already using OpenAI
for your application, it's

121
00:05:13.579 --> 00:05:16.582
not a lot of extra work
or code to add support for structure.

122
00:05:16.582 --> 00:05:17.550
Another benefit is

123
00:05:17.550 --> 00:05:18.318
these providers

124
00:05:18.318 --> 00:05:21.521
are always working on new techniques
and improve the quality of these outputs.

125
00:05:21.621 --> 00:05:25.058
Getting JSON from OpenAI has already
improved tremendously in the last year,

126
00:05:25.358 --> 00:05:27.593
There are some drawbacks
to these approaches.

127
00:05:27.593 --> 00:05:31.497
The major one is that your code is now
tied to a very specific model provider.

128
00:05:31.631 --> 00:05:35.268
This means that changing providers
can be a major refactor

129
00:05:35.268 --> 00:05:37.470
if your code is written
to work with OpenAI,

130
00:05:37.470 --> 00:05:39.739
and you want to try experimenting
with Instructor,

131
00:05:39.739 --> 00:05:42.909
you may have to write a significant amount
of your structured code again.

132
00:05:43.142 --> 00:05:45.778
Another issue is inconsistent results.

133
00:05:45.778 --> 00:05:49.649
This depends on the provider, of course,
but some providers do not consistently

134
00:05:49.749 --> 00:05:52.685
return the structure you were expecting,
which can be a major problem

135
00:05:52.685 --> 00:05:54.520
if you're trying to build
scalable systems.

136
00:05:54.520 --> 00:05:57.790
Another issue is the unclear impact
on the quality of the output.

137
00:05:58.057 --> 00:06:01.060
For many evaluations,
there are some people that have found

138
00:06:01.060 --> 00:06:02.495
that working with structured outputs

139
00:06:02.495 --> 00:06:05.932
from certain providers sometimes hurts
the performance on these evaluations.

140
00:06:06.265 --> 00:06:07.500
This is not universal

141
00:06:07.500 --> 00:06:09.469
and it's not necessarily proven,
but this is

142
00:06:09.469 --> 00:06:11.671
something people have suspected
and it can be an issue.

143
00:06:11.671 --> 00:06:14.874
Additionally, there are limited options
for the types of structure

144
00:06:14.874 --> 00:06:15.875
that you can use.

145
00:06:15.875 --> 00:06:19.145
Typically, you're only working
with a subset of proper JSON.

146
00:06:19.512 --> 00:06:22.548
You can get names and fields,
but you can't get detailed,

147
00:06:22.682 --> 00:06:24.083
regular expressions around your field,

148
00:06:24.083 --> 00:06:26.586
such as making sure a date matches
a certain format.

149
00:06:26.586 --> 00:06:29.989
One solution to these problems
is to use what we call re-promoting libraries.

150
00:06:30.189 --> 00:06:33.659
Proprietary models only work
with a specific API, but re-prompting

151
00:06:33.659 --> 00:06:36.662
libraries are designed to work
with any major LLM provider.

152
00:06:36.662 --> 00:06:39.665
Examples of these type of tools
are Instructor and LangChain.

153
00:06:39.732 --> 00:06:41.334
Re-prompting is very interesting.

154
00:06:41.334 --> 00:06:44.604
The way it works
is we start with a regular pass to the LLM,

155
00:06:44.604 --> 00:06:47.140
we provide a prompt
and the one provides an output.

156
00:06:47.140 --> 00:06:50.276
Additionally,
we provide the model with a validator.

157
00:06:50.309 --> 00:06:53.312
This is just a description
of how we expect the JSON to look.

158
00:06:53.646 --> 00:06:58.117
If the output of the model conforms to our
validator, the data sent straight to us.

159
00:06:58.384 --> 00:07:01.187
This is great, but what if there's
a problem in the output?

160
00:07:01.187 --> 00:07:03.523
In this case,
we see there's an angle bracket

161
00:07:03.523 --> 00:07:05.591
being generated
where we expect a curly bracket.

162
00:07:05.591 --> 00:07:07.326
This is not a valid output.

163
00:07:07.326 --> 00:07:10.696
The re-prompting library
will automatically take that information

164
00:07:10.696 --> 00:07:14.434
about why this failed to parse
appended to the prompt, and try again.

165
00:07:14.801 --> 00:07:16.702
There are many upsides to this approach.

166
00:07:16.702 --> 00:07:19.705
One is that
now you can work with any LLM API.

167
00:07:20.139 --> 00:07:23.509
That's a huge step forward in
making great software that can be reused.

168
00:07:23.776 --> 00:07:27.480
This allows for that structure
you wrote code for to be used across APIs.

169
00:07:27.480 --> 00:07:30.483
Oftentimes, switching providers
all it requires is changing the key

170
00:07:30.483 --> 00:07:31.818
and the name of the provider.

171
00:07:31.818 --> 00:07:34.353
We also get greater flexibility
in our structure than we do

172
00:07:34.353 --> 00:07:36.189
with most proprietary providers.

173
00:07:36.189 --> 00:07:37.523
So it's still only JSON,

174
00:07:37.523 --> 00:07:38.257
but we're allowed to use

175
00:07:38.257 --> 00:07:41.928
regexes for the field so we can enforce
constraints like a specific date format,

176
00:07:42.128 --> 00:07:45.131
or making sure that the user's
email address is a valid email address.

177
00:07:45.364 --> 00:07:47.333
There are some drawbacks
to this approach as well.

178
00:07:47.333 --> 00:07:48.234
The biggest one is that

179
00:07:48.234 --> 00:07:51.771
retries can be quite costly,
both in terms of money and time.

180
00:07:51.771 --> 00:07:54.140
For many developers of LLM applications,

181
00:07:54.140 --> 00:07:57.777
time is a bigger consideration because
you're making your users wait for results.

182
00:07:58.010 --> 00:08:01.380
Following from this,
there's not even a guarantee of success.

183
00:08:01.747 --> 00:08:04.684
If after a certain number of retries,
the library is not succeeded,

184
00:08:04.684 --> 00:08:06.052
it will simply fail.

185
00:08:06.052 --> 00:08:07.119
And once again,

186
00:08:07.119 --> 00:08:10.857
while we do have more control over
our structure, we still only have JSON

187
00:08:10.857 --> 00:08:12.091
as a viable output.

188
00:08:12.091 --> 00:08:15.027
Structure generation,
also known as constrain decoding,

189
00:08:15.027 --> 00:08:18.364
is a method for working directly
with the model to get the output we want.

190
00:08:18.498 --> 00:08:21.734
We use the term structured generation
because we are actually controlling

191
00:08:21.734 --> 00:08:24.136
the generation of the tokens
to get our structured output

192
00:08:24.136 --> 00:08:24.871
in the end.

193
00:08:24.871 --> 00:08:28.407
There are many libraries to support this,
including Outlines by .txt,

194
00:08:28.875 --> 00:08:33.546
SGLang, Microsoft's Guidance and XGrammar.
To understand how these work,

195
00:08:33.579 --> 00:08:36.849
let's refresh a little bit on how LLMs
generate tokens.

196
00:08:37.016 --> 00:08:38.084
We start with a prompt,

197
00:08:38.084 --> 00:08:40.286
which is transformed
into a bunch of tokens

198
00:08:40.286 --> 00:08:42.955
that are passed into the LLM
to be processed all at once.

199
00:08:42.955 --> 00:08:44.490
The LLM then transforms

200
00:08:44.490 --> 00:08:48.227
those tokens into a distribution
of what the next token is going to be.

201
00:08:48.327 --> 00:08:52.598
These weights, called logits, describe
how likely each given token is.

202
00:08:52.865 --> 00:08:56.702
A token is then sampled from these weights
and appended to the prompt.

203
00:08:57.103 --> 00:09:00.540
Then, tokens are continued to be sampled
until you reach the end of statement

204
00:09:00.540 --> 00:09:01.407
token.

205
00:09:01.407 --> 00:09:04.043
This is how structure generation
works behind the scenes.

206
00:09:04.043 --> 00:09:07.179
Suppose we want to define our structure
as only allow strings

207
00:09:07.179 --> 00:09:08.614
where each character is in order?

208
00:09:08.614 --> 00:09:11.651
We'll talk a bit later on in the course
about how we actually do that.

209
00:09:11.651 --> 00:09:14.353
But for now, just assume
that this is the constraint we have.

210
00:09:14.353 --> 00:09:17.356
Valid strings under this structure
would be ABC,

211
00:09:17.623 --> 00:09:21.761
AABC, AC, and BBB. And all of those cases,

212
00:09:21.761 --> 00:09:23.162
each letter is in order.

213
00:09:23.162 --> 00:09:26.866
Invalid examples would be BAC and CCCA.

214
00:09:27.133 --> 00:09:29.135
Both of those have characters
that are out of order

215
00:09:29.135 --> 00:09:31.404
and therefore don't follow
the rules of our structure.

216
00:09:31.404 --> 00:09:33.472
So let's walk through
how this actually works.

217
00:09:33.472 --> 00:09:36.275
Look at this distribution of logits here
for our first character.

218
00:09:36.275 --> 00:09:39.345
We actually have no constraints
because any character can be a valid

219
00:09:39.345 --> 00:09:42.048
first character. Let's assume we sample b.

220
00:09:42.048 --> 00:09:44.150
Now we have new logits
that are generated

221
00:09:44.150 --> 00:09:46.819
as B is appended to our prompt
and the process is rerun.

222
00:09:46.819 --> 00:09:49.589
But these logits do not work
for our constraints

223
00:09:49.589 --> 00:09:52.491
because there's a certain amount
of probability assigned to A.

224
00:09:52.491 --> 00:09:55.127
A is not valid
according to the rules of our structure.

225
00:09:55.127 --> 00:09:57.697
And this is how structure
generation works.

226
00:09:57.697 --> 00:10:00.399
It actually modifies
those logits, removing

227
00:10:00.399 --> 00:10:03.970
the ones that are invalid and reweighting
the ones that are allowed.

228
00:10:04.203 --> 00:10:05.438
We animate another token,

229
00:10:05.438 --> 00:10:09.241
a C, and once again have new logits
and have the same problem once again.

230
00:10:09.675 --> 00:10:12.678
A and B now, are not valid
next tokens in the sequence.

231
00:10:12.878 --> 00:10:15.247
So once again, structure generation

232
00:10:15.247 --> 00:10:18.250
simply remove those from the possibilities
of what can be sampled.

233
00:10:18.384 --> 00:10:21.487
Read normalizes the probabilities,
and we continue this process

234
00:10:21.487 --> 00:10:24.490
until we finally generate a string
that is guaranteed to adhere

235
00:10:24.657 --> 00:10:26.292
to the rules of our structure.

236
00:10:26.292 --> 00:10:28.728
Let's talk about the pros
and cons of structure generation.

237
00:10:28.728 --> 00:10:30.830
There are many versions of this technique.

238
00:10:30.830 --> 00:10:33.165
It works with any open
LLM out there.

239
00:10:33.165 --> 00:10:36.936
So if you use models from HuggingFace,
any of those models, including models

240
00:10:36.936 --> 00:10:39.672
like Vision models,
will work with structure generation.

241
00:10:39.672 --> 00:10:42.575
Because we are working directly
with the model,

242
00:10:42.575 --> 00:10:44.243
it is extremely fast.

243
00:10:44.243 --> 00:10:46.679
The cost during inference
is basically zero.

244
00:10:46.679 --> 00:10:47.346
In fact,

245
00:10:47.346 --> 00:10:48.848
there is research that it can even be used

246
00:10:48.848 --> 00:10:51.517
to improve inference time
by taking advantage of structure

247
00:10:51.517 --> 00:10:53.552
that is inherent in tokens
that can be skipped.

248
00:10:53.552 --> 00:10:56.055
It provides higher quality results.

249
00:10:56.055 --> 00:10:57.857
You can check out our blog
for more details.

250
00:10:57.857 --> 00:11:00.826
But over and over again,
when we have run evaluations head to head

251
00:11:00.926 --> 00:11:03.629
between structured generation
and non-structured generation,

252
00:11:03.629 --> 00:11:06.632
we have found structure generation
improves benchmark performances.

253
00:11:06.832 --> 00:11:10.102
This also works very well in resource
constrained environments.

254
00:11:10.236 --> 00:11:12.571
Because it's very lightweight
and very efficient,

255
00:11:12.571 --> 00:11:15.574
even on very small devices
that have tiny LLMs,

256
00:11:15.608 --> 00:11:18.978
you can still use structured generation
to guarantee the outputs of your model,

257
00:11:19.245 --> 00:11:23.082
And we can produce a huge range
of structure of course, JSON,

258
00:11:23.249 --> 00:11:26.352
but also regular expressions
or even syntactically correct code.

259
00:11:26.619 --> 00:11:28.821
There is a drawback to working
with structured generation,

260
00:11:28.821 --> 00:11:31.390
and because we are using the logits
directly,

261
00:11:31.390 --> 00:11:34.060
it means you need to have control
over those logits.

262
00:11:34.060 --> 00:11:37.997
So, that means either
you're working with open models, or you

263
00:11:37.997 --> 00:11:41.934
yourself are hosting proprietary model
and you control the logits. Another way

264
00:11:41.934 --> 00:11:42.535
to view the things

265
00:11:42.535 --> 00:11:46.605
we've discussed, is this is the journey
from prompt hacking to AI engineering.

266
00:11:46.772 --> 00:11:50.509
When we're working with just chat
based interfaces, we are really doing

267
00:11:50.509 --> 00:11:52.011
the epitome of prompt hacking.

268
00:11:52.011 --> 00:11:54.747
We are changing the messages
we sent, the LLM.

269
00:11:54.747 --> 00:11:56.382
We are interpreting the results,

270
00:11:56.382 --> 00:11:59.085
and we keep iterating on this process
until we're happy with the output.

271
00:11:59.085 --> 00:12:01.854
But this is not scalable.
This is not even repeatable.

272
00:12:01.854 --> 00:12:04.256
So we're very far from real software
at this point.

273
00:12:04.256 --> 00:12:08.427
Moving to proprietary Json APIs opens up
a whole new world of possibility for us.

274
00:12:08.461 --> 00:12:10.463
We can now start writing real software.

275
00:12:10.463 --> 00:12:13.833
We're getting predictable responses
from our model that allow us to integrate

276
00:12:13.833 --> 00:12:18.104
code using an LLM into other parts
of our software. But there is a hang up,

277
00:12:18.304 --> 00:12:21.307
most of our code for doing
this is tied to a single provider.

278
00:12:21.507 --> 00:12:25.077
Re-prompting libraries
solve this by making it possible for us

279
00:12:25.077 --> 00:12:29.381
to create reusable software libraries
that can use a variety of different

280
00:12:29.381 --> 00:12:30.716
LLM providers.

281
00:12:30.716 --> 00:12:35.121
This is a huge step forward in creating
scalable software that works with LLMs.

282
00:12:35.488 --> 00:12:40.292
However, there's still a big gap
between our code and the model itself.

283
00:12:40.526 --> 00:12:44.096
We're relying on this clever
but limited prompting trick

284
00:12:44.096 --> 00:12:45.431
to get the results we need,

285
00:12:45.431 --> 00:12:49.668
and that's where structure generation
is really arriving at true AI engineering.

286
00:12:49.869 --> 00:12:53.539
We're working directly with the model
to get exactly the results we want.

287
00:12:53.606 --> 00:12:56.041
We can write code
that is easy to understand,

288
00:12:56.041 --> 00:12:58.277
easy to scale, and easy to improve.

289
00:12:58.277 --> 00:13:02.148
So in this lesson, we covered the basics
of what structured outputs are.

290
00:13:02.615 --> 00:13:06.018
We learned why they're useful and how
they allow us to write scalable software.

291
00:13:06.118 --> 00:13:09.054
We also learned about the different
varieties of structured outputs

292
00:13:09.054 --> 00:13:10.489
there are. Including

293
00:13:10.489 --> 00:13:14.660
vendor provided APIs, re-prompting
libraries, and structure generation.

294
00:13:14.794 --> 00:13:17.930
Lastly, we saw how all of this comes
together to make it possible

295
00:13:17.930 --> 00:13:20.933
to create truly amazing software
using LLMs.

296
00:13:21.100 --> 00:13:24.069
In the next lesson,
you'll see how this all works by building

297
00:13:24.069 --> 00:13:28.140
a social media agent
using OpenAI's structured Outputs API.

298
00:13:28.507 --> 00:13:30.142
Stay tuned and see you at the next lesson.

